//? Title: DDA Line Drawing: Actual Accumulators, Rational Samples and Visible Rounding Limits
//? Subtitle: Preserve the original shallow and steep examples while checking ties, drift, work and full-width geometry
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Rasterizing an integer-endpoint segment means choosing pixel centres near a
continuous line. A major-axis sampling rule gives one position per column
or row, but rounding and floating accumulation determine which minor-axis
centre is selected. This report derives the digital differential analyzer
(DDA), begins with a five-sample example, and retains the original fifteen-
and eleven-centre rasters. Native traces expose the actual double values
before rounding, while an integer rational-sample API supplies an independent
mathematical comparison. Tiny accumulated errors change exact halfway choices;
large coordinate origins can produce errors greater than half a pixel.
We distinguish these effects from Bresenham's directed ties, measure actual
additions and rounds, and check complete allocation limits and full-width
endpoint arithmetic without attempting an enormous allocation.
)

\toc

> Five samples make the rounding rule visible

For (0,0)→(4,2), the major extent is four and the exact increments are
(1,1/2). These increments are representable in binary, so the actual
accumulators agree with the exact rational samples. At x=1 and x=3, the
height lies halfway between two integer rows. This API rounds halfway away
from zero, selecting heights one and two. The diagram uses equal coordinate
scales; filled blue centres are returned pixels, and hollow red marks are
the actual pre-rounding half-height samples. Points near the line need not
lie on it, correcting the original report's description of rounded samples.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dda_samples.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,4,2);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(160,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke grid;grid.color={226,232,240};grid.width=1;
  for(int x=0;x<=4;++x)d.path({{double(x),-.25},{double(x),2.25}},grid);
  for(int y=0;y<=2;++y)d.path({{-.25,double(y)},{4.25,double(y)}},grid);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke ideal;ideal.color={148,163,184};ideal.width=3;ideal.dashed=true;d.path({{0,0},{4,2}},ideal);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=2;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke red;red.color={220,38,38};red.width=2;red.arrow=true;
  std::print("| sample i | actual x | actual y | rounded centre | rational nearest centre | y error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& s:t.samples) {
    const auto [x,y]=s.point;d.ellipse({double(x),double(y)},.045,.045,{37,99,235},blue);
    if(s.y!=y) {d.ellipse({s.x,s.y},.04,.04,{255,255,255},red);d.path({{s.x,s.y},{double(x),double(y)-.06}},red);}
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",s.index,s.x,s.y,s.point,s.exact_point,s.y_error);
  }
  if(t.points.size()!=5 || t.differing_samples || t.max_coordinate_error!=.5)return 1;
  d.text({2,2.7},"DDA samples (1, 0.5) apart; blue centres round halfway away from zero",22)
   .text({0,-.15},"(0,0)",18).text({4,2.17},"(4,2)",18)
   .text({1,1.2},"(1,1)",18).text({3,2.2},"(3,2)",18)
   .text({2,-.65},std::format("{} samples; {} coordinate additions; {} rounds; maximum coordinate error {}",t.points.size(),t.coordinate_additions,t.roundings,t.max_coordinate_error),21)
   .save_svg("build/dda_samples.svg");
}
