\caption(The arrows join actual half-height accumulators to their rounded centres. Their lengths are half a grid unit. This binary-exact example illustrates the intended sampling rule before floating drift is introduced.)
\alttext(A dashed line rises from zero-zero to four-two. Blue pixel centres include one-one and three-two. Hollow samples at one-half and three-one-and-a-half connect vertically to their rounded centres.)

> Derive the major-axis recurrence and separate its two errors

For signed endpoint differences Δx and Δy, let N=max(|Δx|,|Δy|). If N=0,
return the single coincident endpoint; dividing by N would be undefined.
Otherwise the exact parameter samples are

\[
p_i=\left(x_0+\frac{i\Delta x}{N},\ y_0+\frac{i\Delta y}{N}\right),
\qquad i=0,\ldots,N,\qquad
h=\left(\frac{\Delta x}{N},\frac{\Delta y}{N}\right).
\]
\alttext(Sample i is the initial point plus i divided by N times the endpoint difference, for all indices zero through N. The exact increment is the difference divided by N.)

At least one component of h is +1 or −1, so that major coordinate visits
each integer between its endpoints once. Both components have magnitude at
most one. The floating implementation computes each increment once, begins
at the integer coordinates represented as doubles, rounds both current
accumulators, and adds the two increments before each nonterminal sample.
The final unused addition in the old loop is omitted without changing its
returned points. These are *two coordinate additions per transition and two
coordinate rounds per sample*, rather than one of each for the whole point.

For exact samples, nearest-integer rounding gives coordinate error at most
one half. The major coordinate has zero error, so the selected centre is
within half a unit of its sampled point and hence of the segment. Consecutive
exact centres are 8-connected and the major coordinate makes them distinct.
For a minor increment strictly smaller than one, nearest rounding cannot
cross two distinct half-integer boundaries in a single step; when its
magnitude is exactly one, the integer start makes every minor sample integer.
Reversing the endpoints enumerates the same rational samples in reverse,
including the same signed tie choices. These are guarantees of
`dda_line_exact`, not universal claims about a double accumulator.

To see the distinction, write the stored increment as h+δ and let ρ_j be
the real error introduced by the jth floating addition in one coordinate.
Then the actual accumulator a_i obeys

\[
a_i-p_i=i\delta+\sum_{j=1}^{i}\rho_j,\qquad
|\operatorname{round}(a_i)-p_i|
\le\frac12+|a_i-p_i|.
\]
\alttext(Accumulation error equals i times the increment error plus the sum of addition errors. Rounded error is no more than half a unit plus accumulation error.)

The first identity follows by expanding the recurrence and cancelling the
exact additions; the inequality is the triangle inequality. Even very small
drift can cross a halfway threshold. Its magnitude also depends on coordinate
scale because the spacing of representable doubles grows with that scale.
The trace retains actual pre-rounding x and y, exact-rational nearest centres,
and approximate error diagnostics. Diagnostics subtract an integer quotient
part first and then the signed fractional remainder, avoiding cancellation
against a large rounded ideal coordinate. They are ordinary double
approximations, not certified error intervals. No accumulator is repaired,
clamped or replaced by its reference, and the final minor endpoint is not
forced into the ordinary result.

> Retain both original rasters with undistorted geometry

The original shallow segment (0,0)→(14,6) returns fifteen centres; its steep
control (0,0)→(3,10) returns eleven. The complete shallow sequence remains
visible in the computed output. Each panel below uses the same horizontal
and vertical unit scale. Joining centres is a visual guide; the API selects
centres, without cell supercoverage, intensity, antialiasing or clipping.
The report code only arranges and labels native trace data.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dda_line.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::array<int,4>> lines{{0,0,14,6},{0,0,3,10}};
  const std::vector<dm::algorithms::DdaTrace> traces{dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,14,6),dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,3,10)};
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(40,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke grid;grid.color={226,232,240};grid.width=1;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke ideal;ideal.color={148,163,184};ideal.width=3;ideal.dashed=true;
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke blue;blue.color={37,99,235};blue.width=2;
  std::print("| original segment | centres | exact-reference differences | max coordinate error | additions | rounds |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<lines.size();++k) {
    const auto& a=lines[k];const auto& t=traces[k];const double shift=k?20:0;
    for(int x=0;x<=a[2];++x)d.path({{shift+x,-.3},{shift+x,double(a[3])+.3}},grid);
    for(int y=0;y<=a[3];++y)d.path({{shift-.3,double(y)},{shift+a[2]+.3,double(y)}},grid);
    d.path({{shift,0},{shift+a[2],double(a[3])}},ideal);
    std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> points;for(const auto& [x,y]:t.points)points.push_back({shift+x,double(y)});
    d.path(points,blue);for(const auto& p:points)d.ellipse(p,.10,.10,{37,99,235},blue);
    d.text({shift+a[2]/2.,a[3]+1.3},std::format("(0,0) to ({},{})",a[2],a[3]),22)
     .text({shift+a[2]/2.,-1.2},std::format("{} centres; N={}",t.points.size(),t.steps),21);
    if(t.points.size()!=(k?11:15))return 1;
    std::print("| (0,0) to ({},{}) | {} | {} | {:.9f} | {} | {} |\n",a[2],a[3],t.points.size(),t.differing_samples,t.max_coordinate_error,t.coordinate_additions,t.roundings);
  }
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<traces.size();++k)std::print("\nOriginal {} centres: {}.\n",k?"steep":"shallow",traces[k].points);
  d.text({11.5,-3},"Grey dashed: geometric segment. Blue: actual accumulated-DDA centres. Equal x/y scales.",21)
   .save_svg("build/dda_line.svg");
}
