> Observe a missed tie, a reversal difference and coordinate-scale drift

For (0,0)→(12,1), the exact midpoint height is 1/2. Repeated additions of
1/12 put the current double slightly below that value, so rounding returns
row zero, while exact signed halfway rounding returns row one. Both centres
are half a unit from that *exact* midpoint, so this example shows a tie-rule
mismatch rather than a strict geometric-error violation.

The segment (0,0)→(10,7) exposes direction dependence of accumulation: the
forward and reverse additions approach the same rational height 7/2 from
different floating sides. The exact-sample API remains reversal symmetric.
Its tie policy differs from the existing Bresenham implementation: for the
reverse segment (2,1)→(0,0), exact signed rounding keeps (1,1), while
Bresenham's directed tie chooses (1,0). Negative halves round away from zero,
so integer translations across zero can also change exact tie selections.
None of these policies is claimed to be universally interchangeable.

Finally, translate the minor coordinate of a 10,000-step height-one line
close to INT_MAX. Increment quantization during addition now produces a
visible count of reference differences and a maximum coordinate error
strictly greater than half a pixel. The trace computes this diagnostic
against rational pieces; it does not obtain it by subtracting two almost
equal large ideal-coordinate doubles. The displayed decimal accumulators
are observations from this build under ordinary floating evaluation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dda_rounding.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <climits>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,12,1),f=dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,10,7),r=dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(10,7,0,0);
  const auto small=dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,10000,1),large=dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,INT_MAX-1,10000,INT_MAX);
  if(t.samples[6].point==t.samples[6].exact_point || f.samples[5].point==r.samples[5].point || large.max_coordinate_error<=.5)return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(70,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=5.3;box.height=1.9;box.font_size=21;box.fill={219,234,254};
  d.node("acc",{0,4},std::format("Actual 12-step midpoint\ny = {:.17g}\nrounded {}",t.samples[6].y,t.samples[6].point),box);
  box.fill={220,252,231};d.node("ref",{8,4},std::format("Exact rational midpoint\ny = 1/2\nrounded {}",t.samples[6].exact_point),box);
  box.fill={219,234,254};d.node("forward",{0,0},std::format("10-step forward midpoint\ny = {:.17g}\nrounded {}",f.samples[5].y,f.samples[5].point),box);
  box.fill={254,226,226};d.node("reverse",{8,0},std::format("10-step reverse midpoint\ny = {:.17g}\nrounded {}",r.samples[5].y,r.samples[5].point),box);
  d.text({4,6},"Rounding actual accumulators and rational samples can select different centres",22)
   .text({4,2},"Same rational height 7/2; opposite traversal accumulates differently",21)
   .text({4,-2},std::format("Large-origin control: {} changed samples; max coordinate error {:.6f}",large.differing_samples,large.max_coordinate_error),22)
   .save_svg("build/dda_rounding.svg");
  std::print("| control | sample | actual y | actual centre | rational nearest | y drift |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [label,s]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,dm::algorithms::DdaSample>>{{"12-step midpoint",t.samples[6]},{"10-step forward",f.samples[5]},{"10-step reverse",r.samples[5]},{"large-origin midpoint",large.samples[5000]}})
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.17g} | {} | {} | {:.9g} |\n",label,s.index,s.y,s.point,s.exact_point,s.y_drift);
  std::print("\nOrigin comparison: low max error {:.6f}, changed samples {}; large max error {:.6f}, changed samples {}.\n",small.max_coordinate_error,small.differing_samples,large.max_coordinate_error,large.differing_samples);
  std::print("Reverse (2,1)->(0,0): exact midpoint {}; Bresenham midpoint {}. Negative half: {}.\n",dm::algorithms::dda_exact_sample(2,1,0,0,1),dm::algorithms::bresenham_line(2,1,0,0)[1],dm::algorithms::dda_exact_sample(0,0,2,-1,1));
}
