\caption(The first row separates a tiny accumulated tie mismatch from exact rational selection. The second shows a different accumulated choice under reversal. The large-origin table/diagnostic additionally exhibits coordinate error greater than one half.)
\alttext(The twelve-step midpoint accumulates slightly below one half and rounds to six-zero, whereas the rational reference selects six-one. Forward and reverse ten-step midpoints round to five-four and five-three. A large-origin diagnostic reports its computed error and changed-sample count.)

> Exact rational selection without a wider-than-64-bit core product

For one coordinate, write Δ=s d with d≥0 and s∈{−1,+1}. At sample i, divide
the unsigned product d i by N into quotient q and remainder r:

\[
d i=qN+r,\quad 0\le r<N,\qquad
p_i=(x_0+s q)+s\frac rN.
\]
\alttext(The unsigned distance times index is quotient times N plus remainder. The exact coordinate is the integer initial value plus signed quotient, followed by the signed remainder fraction.)

The integer part stays within endpoint bounds. If 2r<N, keep it; if 2r>N,
move one unit in direction s. If 2r=N, move only when that direction points
away from zero at the integer part. This last condition rounds the *whole
signed coordinate*, rather than incorrectly rounding the unsigned offset
and then translating it. For all 32-bit int endpoints, d and i are at most
2^32−1, so their product fits uint64; the doubled remainder also fits.
The production quantizer needs no signed overflowing product, floating
selection or nonstandard integer type. A separate test oracle uses a signed
128-bit weighted-endpoint numerator, supplying a different arithmetic route.

> Count actual work and reject whole oversized outputs

For N nonterminal transitions, this implementation performs 2N coordinate
additions and 2(N+1) rounds, returning N+1 centres. Total counted coordinate
operations are 4N+2. The trace's rational comparisons, diagnostics, vector
allocation and copies are additional work. The ordinary API omits trace and
reference calculations. Both output size and running time are O(N+1); only
the recurrence's auxiliary state is O(1), so the old report's constant-space
claim does not describe the returned vector. Coincidence still costs one
point and two rounds, with no additions.

`dda_line_trace` and `dda_line_exact` default to a complete one-million-point
cap; a positive cap is inclusive and checked before allocation, while zero
disables that optional cap. Container capacity checks remain. The legacy
four-argument `dda_line` has no optional cap. Every coordinate difference is
widened before subtraction, and sample/count loops cannot overflow an int.
An ordinary rounded accumulator outside int now raises overflow_error before
conversion; it is neither silently wrapped nor clipped. A successful call
returns its complete sequence, and errors return no partial raster.
The random-access exact sample API lets us inspect enormous spans without
walking or allocating them. No external dataset is appropriate for these
geometric and arithmetic controls.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dda_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <climits>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> sizes,points,operations,theory;
  std::print("| N | points | actual coordinate adds | actual rounds | sum | 4N+2 | max drift |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int n:{0,8,16,32,64,128,256,512,1024}) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,n,n/2);const auto sum=t.coordinate_additions+t.roundings;
    if(sum!=4*static_cast<std::uint64_t>(n)+2 || t.differing_samples || t.max_accumulator_drift!=0)return 1;
    sizes.push_back(n);points.push_back(t.points.size());operations.push_back(sum);theory.push_back(4*n+2);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,t.points.size(),t.coordinate_additions,t.roundings,sum,4*n+2,t.max_accumulator_drift);
  }
  int rejected=0;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,14,6,14);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::dda_line_exact(0,0,14,6,14);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(INT_MIN,0,INT_MAX,1);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::dda_line_exact(INT_MIN,0,INT_MAX,1);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::dda_exact_sample(0,0,12,1,13);}catch(const std::out_of_range&){++rejected;}
  const auto count=dm::algorithms::dda_point_count(INT_MIN,0,INT_MAX,1);
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::dda_exact_sample(INT_MIN,0,INT_MAX,1,2147483647ULL),b=dm::algorithms::dda_exact_sample(INT_MIN,0,INT_MAX,1,2147483648ULL);
  if(rejected!=5 || count!=4294967296ULL || dm::algorithms::dda_line_trace(0,0,14,6,15).points.size()!=15 || dm::algorithms::dda_line_exact(0,0,14,6,0).size()!=15)return 1;
  std::print("\nComplete point caps and sample index: {} expected rejections. Exact inclusive cap 15 succeeds; zero-disabled cap also succeeds.\n",rejected);
  std::print("Full int span: {} inclusive samples; two middle exact centres {} and {}. Count and random access allocate no raster.\n",count,a,b);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(sizes,points,"output points",{220,38,38},2).line(sizes,operations,"adds + rounds",{37,99,235},2)
   .points(sizes,theory,"4N + 2",{31,41,55},4).legend({"output points","adds + rounds","4N + 2"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235},{31,41,55}})
   .title("Counted coordinate work matches 4N + 2 exactly")
   .x_label("major-axis steps N").y_label("returned centres or counted coordinate operations")
   .x_limits(0,1100).y_limits(0,5000).size(1100,580).save_svg("build/dda_work.svg");
}
