> Verification and limits

Nine new native tests pass AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
All 14,641 endpoint quadruples in the signed grid −5…5 compare exact rasters
with the independent signed-128-bit weighted-endpoint oracle, and verify
actual accumulator replay, diagnostics, work, endpoint inclusion, exact
reversal and 8-connectivity. One thousand seeded short lines at varied
coordinate origins check additional octants and drift. Another 25,000 full-
range endpoint/index samples and 2,500 extreme-grid/index combinations check
random access without allocating huge rasters. Tests retain both original
rasters, signed global ties, the measured halfway and reversal counterexamples,
the translated half-pixel violation, coincidence at int extremes, ownership,
complete caps and invalid indices. A five-million-step control near INT_MAX
confirms that actual accumulated overshoot raises overflow_error before an
out-of-range conversion; its exact endpoint sample remains valid. Both directions of the twelve-step example miss its tie in the same
direction, while the ten-step height-seven example exposes the traversal
difference.

The native tests support the implementation; the derivation explains the
mathematical rational rule. The accumulated API deliberately preserves its
original floating recurrence, including visible drift and tie differences.
The exact comparison API supplies a separate precise sampling contract.
Neither implements clipping, antialiasing, supercover rasterization or a GPU
rasterization specification. No timing experiment here establishes that
floating DDA is slower than integer Bresenham on a particular machine; the
report compares arithmetic, rounding contracts and measured counts instead.

> References

- Colin Flanagan, *Line Drawing on Raster Displays*, sections “Digital Differential Analyser” and “Avoiding Duplicate Plotting”. [Author's teaching material](https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/group/goa/mallinnus/lines/gsoft2.html). The implementation uses the incremental major-axis idea, with both coordinates, doubles, inclusive sampling and signed halfway-away-from-zero rounding; the cited code uses different rounding and screen-coordinate conventions.
- Jack E. Bresenham (1965), *Algorithm for computer control of a digital plotter*, IBM Systems Journal 4(1), 25–30. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.1147/sj.41.0025). The comparison uses this library's documented directed all-octant variant.
